WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.735
Module 10 is agents, skills, and workflows — the tools for packaging your own setup so you don't rebuild it by hand every time.

2
00:00:05.735 --> 00:00:07.000
First up: creating an agent.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.230
Automation, Agents, New agent.

4
00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:14.828
Six fields make up an agent: a name, its instructions, which model runs it, which tools it can use, which knowledge bases it can search, and a working directory.

5
00:00:14.828 --> 00:00:17.000
Set them once, and every run reuses the same package.

6
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.109
Here's the difference that decides whether an agent is actually useful.

7
00:00:20.109 --> 00:00:23.569
Instructions that say You review code produce vague, generic output every time.

8
00:00:23.569 --> 00:00:29.000
A real spec — what to check, what severity levels mean, what format to reply in — produces a review you can actually act on.

9
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.007
The worked example: a code-reviewer agent.

10
00:00:31.007 --> 00:00:38.893
Its instructions name exactly what to check — bugs, security issues, style — rate each finding Blocking, Minor, or Nit, and reply as a bulleted list grouped by file.

11
00:00:38.893 --> 00:00:42.000
Give it read access to the repo and nothing else it doesn't need.

12
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.073
Once it's saved, run it straight from the Agents panel — no chat required.

13
00:00:46.073 --> 00:00:54.000
Point it at a file or folder, and it works through your spec exactly the same way every time, because the instructions never drift between runs.

14
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.667
Running an agent standalone is one path.

15
00:00:55.667 --> 00:01:00.000
Next we call one from inside an ordinary conversation, and look at the separate memory each agent keeps.

